<!-- ===== SECTION 6: PROOF STORY ===== -->
<h2>What actually happened over eight weeks</h2>

<p>I ordered them that evening, and I want to be honest about my state of mind: I did not believe it. I had a $4,500 surgical quote in a drawer and I was buying a box of patches, and the thought I actually had was <em>well, it is cheaper than the consultation fee.</em></p>

<p class="beat">Night 1</p>
<p>A light prickle for the first minute or two, then nothing. I forgot they were on and fell asleep. In the morning the area looked smoother and I immediately told myself I was imagining it.</p>

<p class="beat">Week 1</p>
<p>The smoothness was real but it came and went. Better on the mornings after a patch, back to normal by the third day. At this point I assumed I had bought a slightly more elaborate cooling patch and that would be the end of it.</p>

<p class="beat">Week 3</p>
<p>Nothing dramatic — and I want to leave that in, because it is the truth. What I did notice was smaller and stranger: my concealer stopped disappearing. I put it on at 7:30 and it was still doing its job at 2pm. I had not changed the concealer. I had stopped needing the primer.</p>

<p class="beat">Week 5</p>
<p>The morning puffiness started resolving faster. Not absent — resolving. What used to still be there at eleven was gone by eight.</p>

<p class="beat">Week 7</p>
<p>My daughter, who is not a person given to compliments about skincare, looked at me across the kitchen and asked what I had changed. That was the first time somebody else said it out loud.</p>

<p class="beat">Week 8</p>
<p>I left the camera on for an entire meeting and did not think about it once. I only realised afterwards, in the car, that I had not checked my own square the whole time.</p>

<p>I want to be precise about what did and did not happen, because I have read enough of these articles to know how they usually end.</p>

<p><strong>I do not have the under-eyes I had at thirty.</strong> The area is smoother, the shadow is lighter, and the texture that used to catch every harsh light has softened considerably. But I still have a slight fullness in the mornings, and I expect I always will, because some of mine is inherited — my mother has exactly the same eyes and so does my brother.</p>

<p>What changed is that it stopped being the first thing I see. And the quote is out of the drawer.</p>

<p>The wedding was in August. There are photographs. I have looked at all of them and I did not once go looking for the shadow.</p>

