<!-- ===== SECTION 7: OBJECTIONS ===== -->
<h2>The questions I asked before I ordered</h2>

<div class="note"><b>Testimonial block goes here.</b> Per the guide's method, you need roughly five, each doing a specific job: one handling the age objection, one handling the male segment, one handling a mild case (to widen the market beyond severe), one handling the "I've tried everything" objection, and one purely emotional. <b>These must be real customers with real permission.</b> If you do not have five yet, run this page with none and add them as they arrive — an empty slot costs you a little; a fabricated one costs you the account.</div>

<div class="faq">
  <p>"Does it hurt?"</p>
  <p>A light prickle for the first minute or two while the cones soften, then you stop noticing. The cones are far too short to reach a nerve ending. Sharp pain is not normal — if you feel that, take the patch off.</p>
</div>

<div class="faq">
  <p>"Is this just an expensive hydrogel patch?"</p>
  <p>No, and the difference is the whole point. A hydrogel patch holds a serum against the surface of your skin, where the barrier stops most of it. These patches place the actives below that barrier. Same category on a shelf, completely different mechanism.</p>
</div>

<div class="faq">
  <p>"Why has my dermatologist never mentioned this?"</p>
  <p>Mine had not either. Dermatology appointments are short and triaged toward medical issues — moles, rashes, prescriptions. Cosmetic delivery methods rarely come up unless you ask directly. It is not a conspiracy, it is a fifteen-minute appointment.</p>
</div>

<div class="faq">
  <p>"I have very sensitive skin."</p>
  <p>Patch test on your inner forearm and wait 24 hours. If you have eczema, dermatitis or rosacea around the eye, or any broken skin, skip this product entirely. Mild pink that fades within half an hour after removal is expected. Swelling, itching or burning is not — stop and email them.</p>
</div>

<div class="faq">
  <p>"Will it work on bags I have had since I was young?"</p>
  <p>Partly, and it depends what is under there. Fluid, thin skin and lost firmness respond well. Inherited fat pads — the kind visible in photographs of you at twenty — do not move for any topical. Only surgery addresses those. Most people are a mix, which is exactly why two women the same age get different results.</p>
</div>

<div class="faq">
  <p>"<mark>$XX</mark> seems low for something that works."</p>
  <p>I thought so too, and I asked. The honest answer is that the cost sits in the manufacturing of the patch itself, and there is no clinic overhead, no practitioner time, and no consultation fee attached to it. You are buying a product, not an appointment.</p>
</div>

